A||||29||Voyd||winter driving||||1||
Z||000000||Voyd||12-12-1999||02:35 PM||||so we just had our first big storm of the year yesterday. violent winds and slippery snow. i unfortunately had no choice to leave the house and this was the first time i drove a small sports car, or a mid engined car in winter. i just about did a 180 turning into a street. damn this car wants to fishtail so badly.... guess itll just take some getting used to and also to go very slowly. im always a cautious driver but with this car you need to be over-cautious. (plus, i wouldnt wanna wreck it!)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||12-12-1999||04:14 PM||||Voyd, <BR>Not really sure where you live. I suspect, from reading your post, that snow is fairly common there. We get snow on the roads in Atlanta only once in two or three years. One inch is enough to shut down the entire city. If it wasn't such a pain in the @$$, it would be really funny. Of course, there are those people who say that we (in Atlanta) don't know how to drive on dry pavement. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||2FAST4U||12-12-1999||04:55 PM||||Voyd;<P>I live in Denver and drive a Fiero in the snow often.<BR>I've found the Fiero to be very good in the snow as long as common sence and a few general rules are used.<P>1. good tires<P>2. use the highest gear possible for the speed. this will help to keep from spinning the rear tires.<P>3. Look as far ahead as possible to anticipated your next move - try to brake or accelerate in a straight line - brake before a curve and acclerate after the curve - use a nutural throttle in the curve.<P>4. Respect the conditiions.<P>5. Be prepared ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voyd||12-12-1999||04:56 PM||||im near Montreal. it snows a heck of a lot so whenever possible i will leave the little car in the garage, where it should stay in the winter. pity i have to take it out in the slush and snow. sure beats having to be on foot during the winter. my buddy has a very nice Trans-Am which he doesnt take out in winter. poor sucker is on foot now tho, lol.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Voyd (edited 12-12-1999).]||||reg||8
Z||000004||2FAST4U||12-12-1999||05:09 PM||||I can understand why he parks hi Trans Am in the winter. I used to have a 90 IROC. That Camaro got sideways as soon as the weatherman mentioned snow on the radio.||||reg||1
Z||000005||overlord||12-12-1999||07:44 PM||||this is the first year im gonna drive my fiero in the winter and i must say i am a nervous nelly.<P>thank god there isnt any snow yet and this year is exceptionally warm , but there was a little skiff of snow one day and geez, i did a 180 with just a normal turn...so yes you have to drive extra extra careful..||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||12-12-1999||11:52 PM||||Raydar--I've been to Atlanta many, many times (rush hour traffic on 285...aarrggHH). <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  It's true.  You guys don't know how to drive on dry pavement.  But you don't have anything on Jacksonville, FL.  I was there a few years ago when it actually snowed (I personally saw 14 flakes-the radio called a storm <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).  People went out of their minds!!<P>Voyd--I've been to Montreal, too.  At least you guys up there are prepared for the snow.  There are alot of places in the US (mainly below the Mason-Dixon line) that don't even have salt/sand.  There's one county in Tennessee that I know of for sure that doesn't even have a snowplow.  Honestly <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||ChadMan||12-13-1999||09:47 AM||||Tires make all the difference in the world when it comes to snow driving.  The best snow car I've ever owned was my 84 Fiero with new all-season Dunlop 185/80R13 tires.  I could safely maintain 35 mph in 8 inches of unplowed snow.<P>The worst snow car I've ever owned is my 88 Coupe with new Yokohama 215/60R14's out back and half-shot Dunlop 195/70R14's up front.  In 6 inches of snow, I couldn't even leave my parking lot.  I'm thinking about buying an extra set of wheels and mounting some Bridgestone Blizzaks or something similar.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DJRice||12-13-1999||11:33 AM||||Monkey, <P>You were in Jax for that?  That was a a hectic week for for the residents of J-ville.  Imagine a town split down the middle by the St. Johns river.  Now Imagine all of the bridges being closed because they were iced over.  It was absolutely crazy!<P>The funny thing was, 9 months after that snowy week, local maternity wards were very very busy.  I guess people were doing more than letting thier chestnuts roast over an open fire.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Clint||12-13-1999||02:19 PM||||I've always done really well with Fiero's in the winter... Much better than i expected to.  No problems so far... You just got to keep your head and take it easy.  <BR>Northeast PA, where we tend to get more ice than snow.||||reg||1
Z||000010||stsmithgt||12-13-1999||05:19 PM||||Don't think I'll make it to the snow this year unless a wild hair takes me to tahoe.  The only thing I don't like is the highly used intersections.  When it rains, all that oil dropped on the ground makes it really slippery coming to a stop.  Some intersection are more slippery than others and boy does this fiero slide.  I'm getting better tires.  The rest of the time it's pretty fun powering it through a slide! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000011||Standard||12-14-1999||01:07 AM||||The only reason I'm waiting for winter is so I can go do some doughnuts in my duke!  I love rear-wheel drive!  For normal driving the duke ain't too bad 'cause it doesn't have all the torque of the v6, but I've still looped out at the end of a highway onramp.  That was scary, thought I was gonna go in the ditch.||||reg||6
Z||000012||DJRice||12-14-1999||08:29 AM||||Yup, them doing them donuts is pretty cool.  I spun my Fiero around 360 in some damp grass one day and my Friend with the eclipse was jealous because he has FWD and cant sling it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||12-14-1999||11:18 AM||||Your friend with the Eclipse CAN do donuts.  He just has to do it in reverse.  It's not quite as cool, but it can be done.  <P>Standard--make sure you have a BIG parking lot.  I was doing some donuts in a friends '71 Cuda years ago and accidentally hit a curb with the rear wheel.  Tore the sh** out of his car.  He wasn't a happy camper.  Can you believe he punched me!?!  I *did* learn my lesson, though.||||reg||1
Z||000014||RFMike||12-14-1999||11:31 PM||||Donuts in a FWD are done by hitting a turn and grabbing the parking brake.  You have to keep the release engaged so that you can feather the brake to control the slide.<BR>Hope my sons don't see this!||||reg||1
Z||000015||Standard||12-15-1999||02:38 AM||||You definnatly can do donuts in fwd!  Before I had my fiero I drove my moms 86 Olds Delta 88 (<B>3.8</B> liter v6!).  Throw it in reverse and that thing would spin like no tommorow! that was wild.<P>I used to work with a guy who had one of those little Suzuki X90 Bandit things.  He was pretty handy with the parking brake.  Watched him pull into the parking lot and slid right into a spot on dry pavement.<P>I'm always on the lookout for curbs.. It's a tough little car, though.  There's a pretty steep hill by my house and right at the bottom it turns kinda fast to the left.  I was going down that hill last winter a little too fast and slid right over the curb!  Scared I busted something, but it drove fine and I couldn't find anything busted on it when I looked it over good.<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 12-15-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000016||william_thorniii||12-15-1999||04:33 PM||||Guys,<P>The parking brake slide is/was developed for hard cornering on loose surfaces.  All rally drivers use this technique.  Some autocrossers do too in the really sharp corners.  There is noting quite like pulling your car around a sharp turn at 50 MPH while the front wheels are pulling you and the rear are completly locked.  You get the feel of a 4-wheel power slide with much better control.  But driving down the road sideways in my Fiero is fun too.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000017||dogtired||12-15-1999||04:53 PM||||180s   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>360s   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>THAT WHAT IS ALL ABOUT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000018||2birds||12-15-1999||07:27 PM||||I found out the hard way that enduraflex panels don't flex when it's 20 below zero, it shatters.  I was living in Minnesota at the time, and my winter beater blew out the heater core.  Out came my Plastic Poncho while I was waiting on parts.  I lived in the country, and the road had turned to ice during the day.  To make a long story short, I did a couple of slides in each direction before I spun a 180 and got in a fight with a rock.  The rock won, taking out the passenger side door skin, rocker, and rear quarter.  The mailbox the rock was protecting punched through my rear quarter window, and survived intact.  $1300 damage.||||reg||8
Z||000019||Standard||12-16-1999||01:18 AM||||Wow, that's not good... I heard about that problem before, but I drove mine all last winter here in MN and didn't shatter any panels...  then again, I didn't get into any fights with rocks, either..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000020||SpeedPhreak||12-16-1999||02:16 AM||||MonkeyMan- i WOULD have punched u too, a 71 cuda?  yea, you would have been out cold.<P>i love driving in the winter, well driving in general especially in light to nil traffic (im about to lose my license again & im only 24  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">).  when i was in highschool i cut my teeth on a bright red 74 chevy nova, i litteraly got pulled over almost every week (i lived in a relativly small town)& ran out of gas almost once a week with that.  i havent taken any performance driving classes for cars (sportbikes though <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) but plan on it.  i love rally races & would love to have the drivers skillz.  i am actually considering a rally car for a project.  i live in colorado springs colorado but lived in dever for 5 years. the springs is about 75 miles south of denver & our snow melts quite a bit faster.  however this year only 2 storms so far & my fiero was/is grounded.  i still have my bike though....if it wasnt for that i would want snow 365 days a year.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||03:17 AM||||Don't get me wrong.  I deserved what I got.  Probably some more, too.  And how can you lose your license "again" when you're only 24?  I've put almost 3,000,000 miles (correct amount of zeros) on the road and I've never lost my license.  Not saying that I'm better than anyone, but I can't figure out how you did it.  Slow down, man.  I'm all for going fast, but be careful.  ||||reg||1
Z||000022||CUTTER||12-18-1999||03:59 PM||||I am only 18 and had my license for 6 months. In that time I have been pulled over 5 times for speeding, wreckless driving, to dark of tint, and spinning the tires. I got my self out of 3 of the 5, got a fine for going 55 in a 35 which was 93.00 and for tint which was 98.00||||reg||1
Z||000023||batboy||12-18-1999||11:23 PM||||Kids!||||reg||1
Z||000024||fiero56||12-18-1999||11:36 PM||||We're not all bad. Well, maybe just cutter <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. I'm 20, been driving since I was 16, and haven't been touched by the police once. They do tend to "follow" me though. Not sure what that's about. I'll be driving down a road, a cop will follow me everywhere. Looks like they always run my plates 'cause I see them talking on their cb's as thy follow. Friends say cops do the same to them as well.<BR>I'm not saying I'm a perfect driver, I have had my share of cutting it close to getting pulled over. But, I prefer to keep my insurance as low as possible. Back when I started driving, I paid everything car related except insurance. Parents paid it, but told me the first time I get a ticket, I'm paying for insurance from then on. I'd say it really helped keep me in line.<P>Back in high school it was the norm for parents to buy their kids nice brand new cars(vette's, SLK Mercedes, BMW, etc.) Seemed like every month the kid would wreck the car, and mom and dad would buy them another. I don't think thats teaching responsibility very well.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Monkeyman||12-19-1999||12:44 AM||||I wasn't gonna say anything to Cutter, but since batboy made a little comment, here's my 2 cents worth.  ***Cutter, no flames intended***  There is never an excuse for reckless driving, ever.  Some states call it reckless endangerment for good reason.  And going 55 in a 35 is too fast.  I have to assume you were in town.  There are just too many hazards (to both yourself and to others) to speed in town.  I've put almost   3 million miles on the road, so I've seen some pretty ugly things.  What happens when some idiot pulls out in front of you?  What happens when it's a little kid?  Dropping the hammer out on the interstate is a different matter.  As far as the tickets for the tint and "woofing the hoops" (not my phrase, but I kinda like it <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">), those seem kind of bogus, although I can understand the cops p.o.v.  As a matter of fact, I plan on getting a ticket for that sometime in the spring.  I'll get off of my soapbox now.||||reg||1
Z||000026||DJRice||12-20-1999||10:27 AM||||If I recall correctly, I think he already caught some stuff here on the forum for that 55 in a 35 incident.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I can't believe that someone got fined almost  $100 for having tint to dark!  That guy was just itching to write a citation because that is "penny ante" crap.  I have only rarely heard of people getting messed with about their tint unless they were already pulled over for something else.  Florida Cops have better things to do.<P>I can safely say that none of my vehicles have the windows tinted to dark.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Not my Fiero, Not My Jeep, Not my Buick...||||reg||1
Z||000027||ChadMan||12-20-1999||10:43 AM||||How about 108 in a 65?  Is that too fast?||||reg||6
Z||000028||Monkeyman||12-20-1999||12:49 PM||||Uh, huh.  (Unless you're on a race track.  Of course, there probably wouldn't be a speed limit then, would there?)||||reg||1
Z||000029||86_Fastback||12-20-1999||04:11 PM||||Be prepared.  I store my 98 TA and drive my 86 Fiero GT in the winter.  People think I am nuts.  I just prefer rwd vehicles and know how to drive in bad weather.  Tires and thinking ahead are important.<BR>-Gregg||||reg||1
